    "narrative": "For Ghost Ride the WHIP today, the offensive engine is anchored by **James Wood** (78.42) and **Mickey Moniak** (75.75), both of whom capitalize on favorable matchups against vulnerable arms. **James Wood** is our standout play; he benefits from a potent combination of a 4.76 xERA opposing starter and a significant park factor boost, making him a prime candidate for power production. Similarly, **Mickey Moniak** is in an elite spot against Mike Paredes, with a +19% SP skill advantage and a +14% platoon edge driving his score into the high 70s. **Jose Caballero** also warrants attention; while he faces a decent arm, his 62.42 score is propped up by a massive 13% platoon boost and his tendency to run against a slower pitcher, which is vital given our emphasis on the scarcest category, stolen bases.\n\nThe most difficult decision for the lineup optimizer involved **Zach McKinstry**, who narrowly missed the cut with a 47.14 efficiency rating. While **Zach McKinstry** possesses the situational versatility to produce, he was edged out by the superior floor provided by **Kyle Karros** (51.16) and the upside of our middle-of-the-order hitters. We remain cautious regarding the risks inherent in today's \"Lineup Pending\" status for many of our starters, particularly **Austin Riley** (48.28), whose score is suppressed by a difficult matchup against Logan Webb and negative BvP data. Furthermore, while **Corbin Carroll** (61.58) provides essential speed, he faces a tough path against Cole Sulser; we are monitoring weather reports, as the wind-out conditions at his park are the only factor keeping his ceiling from being even higher. Finally, **Gabriel Moreno** and **Seiya Suzuki** occupy marginal spots today, serving as high-floor options while we wait for official confirmation on the batting orders to ensure they remain in advantageous slots.",
